          %p Of course, the terms are even less standardised here. Certainly it is common for ‘countable’ to mean countably indexed, and yet it may also (as in classical mathematics) mean denumerable. In at least some contexts, ‘enumerable’ (without the initial ‘d’) means denumerably indexed.
          %p
            Not only does the difference between ‘denumerable’ and ‘countable’ disappear when we add the prefix ‘sub’ (for obvious reasons), but it almost disappears when we add the suffix ‘ly indexed’! Specifically, a set is denumerably indexed if and only if it is both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            and countably indexed; and a set is countably indexed if and only if it is either
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty
            or denumerably indexed. That countability involves
            %em lower
            subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            is also almost irrelevant here; any
            %em inhabited
            detachable subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            is denumerably indexed regardless of its closure under order.
          %p
            On the other hand, it is key that countability involves
            %em detachable
            subsets of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computable+numbers"} computable numbers
            is subcountably indexed, while the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            is uncountable (and thus not countably indexed); yet in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russian+constructivism"} Russian constructivism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrej+Bauer"} Andrej Bauer
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Hanson"} James Hanson
            have showed that there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            are countably indexed, which they refer to as “countable” in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BauerHanson"} their paper
          %p
            One reason to focus on subcountable sets is that this is about the most general concept that definitely does
            %em not
            hold of the set of real numbers. Every split countably indexed set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is subcountable due to there existing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            from the detachable subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            into
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            In addition, in the presence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            %em denumerably indexed
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em split denumerably indexed
            %em countably indexed
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em split countably indexed
            %em subcountably indexed
            and
            %em split subcountably indexed
            disappear, since every surjection out of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            and every subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
          %p
            Under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BHK+interpretation"} BHK interpretation
            of constructive mathematics, one only has the BHK versions of denumerably indexed, countably indexed, subcountably indexed.
          %h3#uncountable_sets Uncountable sets
          %p
            There are a number of different definitions of an uncountable set, which all differ in the absence of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is
              %strong indenumerable
