          %p Similarly as the case for above, the terms are even less standardised here. Certainly it is common for ‘uncountable’ to mean uncountably indexed, and yet it may also (as in classical mathematics) mean indenumerable.
          %p
            We make the distinction between uncountable and uncountably indexed and between indenumerable and indenumerably indexed because in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+constructive+mathematics"} neutral constructive mathematics
            it is consistent that there exist a set which is both indenumerable and indenumerably indexed. However, similarly to countably indexed sets, in the presence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            %em uncountably indexed
            and
            %em split uncountably indexed
            and
            %em indenumerably indexed
            and
            %em split indenumerably indexed
            disappear.
          %p
            There is a lot of freedom in defining uncountably indexed sets. You can start with countable and require that any function from them to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be non-surjective (in the strong sense used in the definition above). Or you can start with denumerable or denumerably indexed sets and require that any function from them to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be non-surjective, then add the additional clause that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            must be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            without requiring it to be a lower subset, since this is enough to prove
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            inhabited and make the order-closure irrelevant. All of these definitions are equivalent!
          %h2#examples_and_properties Examples and Properties
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            is uncountable. Even in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MacNeille+real+numbers"} MacNeille real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
          %p
            More generally, it is impossible for the real numbers to be both subcountable and countably indexed at the same time; subcountability implies that the real numbers have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+equality"} decidable equality
            because the natural numbers have decidable equality and injections preserve and reflect decidable equality, from which one can derive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WLPO"} WLPO
            allowing one to prove that the real numbers are uncountably indexed by any of the usual arguments, resulting in a contradiction.
          %p
            In fact,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WLPO"} WLPO
            itself is sufficient to prove that the real numbers are uncountably indexed. However, it is consistent that, absent
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WLPO"} WLPO
            %em not
            uncountably indexed (cf
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Bauer22"} Bauer 2022
          %p
            Every denumerable set is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            is uncountable. Even without
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            instead, the power set of an infinite set is uncountable.
          %p
            In contrast, the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+numbers"} algebraic numbers
            is denumerable. Also, the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computable+numbers"} computable numbers
            is subcountably indexed (and infinite, so denumerable in classical mathematics). Combined with the uncountability of the real numbers, this gives the existence of transcendental numbers (constructively) and uncomputable numbers (classically).
          %p
            Classically, every set is countably indexed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/xor"} xor
            uncountably indexed. Even constructively, a countably indexed set cannot be uncountably indexed. In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russian+constructivism"} Russian constructivism
            %em every
            set is subcountably indexed.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            is countable, although not denumerably indexed. Conversely, any uncountable set must be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            is countable, every finitely indexed set is countably indexed, every subfinite set is subcountable, and every subfinitely indexed set is subcountably indexed. Conversely, every uncountable set is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite
          %p
            Classically, a denumerable set is precisely an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite
            countable set; sometimes this is written as a
            = succeed "." do
              %em countably infinite set
          %p
            Classically (using
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+unions+of+countable+sets+are+countable"} countable unions of countable sets are countable
          %h2#history History
          %p
            Arguably,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            as such begins with the 1873 correspondence between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Dedekind"} Richard Dedekind
            on countability. Although they tacitly used
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            throughout, generally speaking, their results were constructive if taken to be about countably indexed sets. They worked out the countability of the algebraic numbers and the uncountability of the real numbers, including both constructive and nonconstructive proofs of the existence of transcendental numbers. (This was the same year that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Hermite"} Charles Hermite
            published a proof that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{e}
            is transcendental, although
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Liouville"} Joseph Liouville
            had shown how to artificially construct transcendental numbers as early as 1851.)
          %p
            In subsequent correspondence with Cantor,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karl+Weierstrass"} Karl Weierstrass
            stressed the usefulness of the countability of the algebraic numbers but downplayed the notion of uncountability. Still, he urged Cantor to publish. Since Weierstrass and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitism"} finitist
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leopold+Kronecker"} Leopold Kronecker
            were senior editors at Krelle's Journal, Cantor wrote his 1874 article carefully. He showed the denumerability of the algebraic numbers explicitly and titled the article as if that were the only subject. He then added a footnote in the proofreading stages, using an
            = succeed "-based" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
          %p
            Cantor's argument was essentially a constructive proof that the set of real numbers is uncountable, as it could apply to any denumerably indexed subset. He tacitly used
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LLPO"} LLPO
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
            of real numbers, which are constructive. He also used a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+cut"} Dedekind cut
            to construct the final transcendental number, and it is this that Kronecker would have most likely objected to, since it is an irreducibly infinite procedure to construct a single number. However, the argument is acceptable to any non-finitist constructivist.
          %p In the correspondence with Dedekind, it was Cantor who first considered and proved the uncountability of the real numbers but Dedekind who first considered and proved the countability of the algebraic numbers, which was the ostensible subject of Cantor's article. Furthermore, it was Dedekind who first wrote the interval-based proof that Cantor used in his footnote that was the real point of the article. However, Cantor gave Dedekind no credit. This strained their relationship for a few years afterward.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+argument"} diagonal argument
            also shows the uncountability of the real numbers (nonconstructively through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor%27s+Theorem"} Cantor's Theorem
            on the cardinality of power sets, constructively through a slight generalization), but Cantor did not publish this until 1891. Interpreted finitistically as an algorithm to construct, from a sequence of real numbers, an approximation of a hypothetical number not in the sequence, the diagonal argument is more efficient; pedagogically, it is easier to explain. However, it was not the first.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+ordinal"} countable ordinal
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+cover"} countable cover
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantor%27s_first_set_theory_article"} Cantor’s first set theory article
          %p which discusses both the origin of the 1874 article and the questions of constructivity that arose in its aftermath.
          %ul
            %li#CCGM24
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Cherubini"}> Felix Cherubini
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freek+Geerligs"} Freek Geerligs
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hugo+Moeneclaey"} Hugo Moeneclaey
              %em A Foundation for Synthetic Stone Duality
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.03203"}> arXiv:2412.03203
              )
          %p
            That in the absence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            it is consistent that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            are denumerably indexed:
          %ul
            %li#Bauer22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrej+Bauer"}> Andrej Bauer
                ,
                %em The countable reals
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4CBFUojXoq4"}> video
                ).
            %li#BauerHanson
              %p
